  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Functional/dissociative seizures (F44.5)</content>. Events have fallen from two or three a month to one in ten weeks, during an acute illness, and none for five weeks, after a full course of CBT-informed psychotherapy. Not on any antiseizure medicine. [S]<list><item>Keep the relapse-prevention plan and diary; booster psychotherapy sessions are available.</item><item>Driving: may resume once he has been free of events for three months, in about eight weeks, if he stays event-free, with the neurologist&apos;s agreement and in line with state licensing rules; he agrees not to drive until then.</item><item>Influenza vaccine today; COVID-19 vaccine at his pharmacy this month.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Major depressive disorder, single episode, moderate (F32.1)</content>. PHQ-9 6, down from 13 (54 percent): a response, not yet remission; item 9 scored 0. [S]<list><item>Continue sertraline 50 mg; consider 75 mg if the PHQ-9 is not below 5 at the next visit.</item><item>Continue for at least six to twelve months after remission.</item><item>Safety plan reviewed; 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline confirmed.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Generalized anxiety disorder (F41.1)</content>. GAD-7 7, down from 14. [S]<list><item>Continue sertraline and the coping skills from therapy.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute viral gastroenteritis (A08.4)</content>. Resolved. [S]<list><item>No further action.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PHQ-9</td><td>Baseline, then at least monthly during acute treatment; every 2 to 4 weeks in continuation</td><td>Completed today</td><td>6, down from 13 (54 percent); item 9 scored 0</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PHQ-9 at least monthly during acute treatment</td><td>Baseline, then at least monthly during acute treatment; every 2 to 4 weeks in continuation</td><td>Deferred</td><td>Missed: after the preventive visit, no PHQ-9 was recorded for ten weeks; the sick visit in between did not repeat it. No harm: no suicidal thoughts were reported at the sick visit, and today&apos;s score shows a response. Corrective action: a monthly PHQ-9 by the care manager, by telephone or the patient portal, between visits until remission</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>GAD-7</td><td>With the PHQ-9 when anxiety accompanies depression</td><td>Completed today</td><td>7, down from 14</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide risk reassessment (C-SSRS Since Last Visit and PHQ-9 item 9)</td><td>Every visit during active treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No suicidal thoughts, intent, plan, or behavior; rifle locked; protective factors stronger</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Event diary: frequency, duration, triggers, warning sensations, and injuries</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>One event in ten weeks, during gastroenteritis; none for five weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>SSRI tolerability check two weeks after starting</td><td>2 weeks after starting and after each dose change</td><td>Deferred</td><td>Missed: the nurse telephone check planned two weeks after starting sertraline was not made. Tolerability was first reviewed at the preventive visit five weeks after the start, when he reported mild nausea in the first week that had settled. No harm: no side effects now and no suicidal thoughts at any contact. Corrective action: the care manager now books the two-week call at the visit where an antidepressant is started or changed</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Adherence and adverse effects</td><td>Every visit during active treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Two doses missed during vomiting; otherwise no missed doses; no side effects</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Antiseizure medicines</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>None needed; emergency card in place</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>CBT-informed psychotherapy for functional seizures</td><td>Weekly during the course</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Course completed; relapse-prevention plan written</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Driving</td><td>Every visit until cleared</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Five weeks free of events; may resume after three months, about eight weeks from now, with the neurologist&apos;s agreement</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Sleep, studies, and first-aid plan</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>About seven hours of sleep; admission test taken with accommodations; back at civic meetings</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Lithium, anticonvulsant mood-stabilizer, or antipsychotic monitoring</td><td>Only on those drugs</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Not taking any; no bipolar features</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Cause of the episodes established by the second visit. Outcome by 1774-04-30: Functional/dissociative seizures documented by video-EEG; epilepsy and syncope excluded [S]</item><item>Antiseizure medicine stopped safely. Outcome by 1774-06-11: Levetiracetam tapered over three weeks and stopped, with no change in events and less irritability [S]</item><item>Events reduced by at least half, then free of events for three months. In progress; target 1774-12-31 [S]</item><item>Depression and anxiety improved by at least 50 percent (PHQ-9 and GAD-7). In progress; target 1774-12-31 [S]</item><item>No suicidal thoughts. In progress; target 1774-12-31 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Immunizations given today: Influenza vaccine, 1774-1775 season [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Guideline basis: CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monograph (2026);